<h2>Why anchor maintenance to property</h2>
<p>Income can be concealed; property is harder to hide. A salaried payer can resign, a businessman can show losses, but a flat, a shop or agricultural land stays on the record. Securing maintenance against property serves three distinct ends: it guarantees a fund for future instalments, it deters evasion because default puts the asset itself at risk, and — where a charge is created and noticed — it survives a transfer of the property to third parties.</p>

<h2>The statutory tools, side by side</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Who it helps</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 25, Hindu Marriage Act, 1955</td><td>Spouse awarded permanent alimony in matrimonial proceedings</td><td>Permits the court to secure the gross or periodical payment, if necessary, by a charge on the respondent's immovable property.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 27, HAMA 1956</td><td>Dependants claiming maintenance from a deceased Hindu's estate</td><td>Declares that the claim is not a charge on the estate unless created by will, decree, agreement "or otherwise" — so the charge must be sought and created.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 28, HAMA 1956</td><td>Dependants whose source-estate is transferred</td><td>Lets the right be enforced against a transferee with notice of the right, or a gratuitous transferee; a bona fide purchaser for value without notice takes free.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 39, Transfer of Property Act, 1882</td><td>Any person with a right to maintenance from the profits of immovable property</td><td>Mirrors the HAMA rule for transfers generally: the right follows the property into the hands of transferees with notice and gratuitous transferees.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Order XXI, CPC / fine-recovery machinery under the BNSS</td><td>Any holder of a maintenance decree or order in arrears</td><td>Attachment and sale of the defaulter's property to realise arrears, independent of any charge.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Getting a charge created in practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Identify the property early</strong> — plead the respondent's immovable assets in the petition and the income affidavit stage, with particulars.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Ask for security expressly</strong> — a prayer under Section 25 HMA (or in a HAMA suit) that the amount be secured by a charge on identified property; courts rarely grant what is not sought.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Justify the necessity</strong> — a record of defaults, concealment of income, attempts to alienate assets or departure abroad is what persuades a court that a bare money order will not do.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Perfect the charge</strong> — have the decree spell out the property with full description, and place the charge on the public record so later purchasers have notice.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Guard against alienation</strong> — where a sale is threatened mid-proceeding, seek interim restraint; a transfer to defeat a maintenance claim invites scrutiny, and transferees with notice take subject to the right.</div>
</div>

<h2>Charge versus attachment: two different shields</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Attachment in execution</h4><p>Available to every order-holder once arrears accrue. It reaches property the defaulter owns at that time, but gives no priority before default and no protection if the asset was sold earlier.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>Charge created by decree</h4><p>Created before default, it earmarks a specific property for the maintenance obligation, binds gratuitous transferees and purchasers with notice, and makes the asset a continuing security for future instalments — not only past arrears.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Points the paying side should know</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>A charge is not automatic and is not punitive: courts weigh necessity, and an honest payment record is the best argument against security.</li>
    <li>A bona fide purchaser for value without notice of the maintenance right is protected; diligence in title searches cuts both ways.</li>
    <li>Security under Section 25 HMA can be varied — like the alimony itself — on a change of circumstances, through the statutory variation machinery.</li>
    <li>Consensual security (a registered agreement charging property or a fixed deposit under lien) is often the cleanest settlement term in mutual consent divorces, and courts routinely act on such undertakings.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article explains the statutory framework in general terms. Whether a charge or security is appropriate in a given case depends on its facts; this is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
